Let $x$ be the number of days the team practices. The team must run 3 miles on the first day and 6 miles every day after that. So, the total distance run by a girl is $3 + 6(x-1)$. The boys' team must run 5 miles every day of practice. So, the total distance run by a boy is $5x$. The coach will order new javelins at the end of the day that each girl's total mileage surpasses each boy's.
Let $d$ be the number of days the team practices.
The distance a girl runs is $3 + 6(d-1) = 3 + 6d - 6 = 6d - 3$.
The distance a boy runs is $5d$.
We want to find the smallest integer $d$ such that $6d - 3 > 5d$.
$6d - 3 > 5d$
$6d - 5d > 3$
$d > 3$
So we need $d \ge 4$.
If $d = 4$, the distance a girl runs is $6(4) - 3 = 24 - 3 = 21$ miles.
The distance a boy runs is $5(4) = 20$ miles.
Since $21 > 20$, the coach will order the new javelins when $d=4$.
Then the total miles run by a girl is $6(4) - 3 = 21$ miles.
